# JellyPass behind Caddy, certificates by DNS-01 through Cloudflare's DNS API.
# One site block per household address. Each address must also be mapped to its household in JellyPass
# (admin site: Groups → Addresses, or PUT /v1/groups/<id>/addresses). See docs/reverse-proxy.md.
{
	email {$ACME_EMAIL}
	# DNS-01: Caddy proves each name through a DNS record, so ports 80/443 need not be reachable from the internet
	# for certificates. Token: a Cloudflare API token with Zone → DNS → Edit on the zone (nothing else).
	acme_dns cloudflare {$CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN}
}

# Every household address goes to the member listener (:8787), never to the admin listener (:8788).
# Caddy's defaults are what JellyPass needs, so there is nothing to add:
# - the client's Host goes upstream unchanged (JellyPass picks the household from it);
# - X-Forwarded-For is set to the client's address and X-Forwarded-Proto to https, replacing whatever the
#   client sent (no `trusted_proxies` here, so nobody's forwarding headers are believed);
# - WebSocket upgrades (/socket) pass through, and responses are streamed, not buffered.
# No `encode`: JellyPass compresses its HTML pages itself and marks them no-transform. If you add `encode`,
# it leaves responses that already have a Content-Encoding alone.
(jellypass_member) {
	reverse_proxy jellypass:8787
}

grove.example.com {
	import jellypass_member
}

heath.example.com {
	import jellypass_member
}

# Optional: Jellyfin's own address, for administrators and the Jellyfin dashboard (household addresses refuse
# administration). Remove it to keep Jellyfin off the internet. Members must not be given this address, and it must
# not be one of the household addresses; list it in JELLYFIN_PUBLIC_URLS so the self-check reports it.
# jellyfin.example.com {
# 	reverse_proxy jellyfin:8096
# }

# Optional: the admin site over HTTPS, from private addresses only (your LAN or VPN). The admin listener is not
# gated by itself; anything that can reach this block can try to sign in. Leave it out to use
# http://127.0.0.1:8788/admin/ on the Docker host (or an SSH tunnel) instead.
# admin.example.com {
# 	@outside not remote_ip private_ranges
# 	respond @outside 403
# 	reverse_proxy jellypass:8788
# }
